WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Authorization decides what an authenticated identity is allowed to do.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:13.880
A user may be signed in and still not be allowed to view another tenant record, approve a payment, change a role, export data, or call an administrative endpoint.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:23.740
Roles, groups, permissions, claims, and scopes are common authorization building blocks, but the names do not matter as much as the enforcement.

4
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.600
The application must check whether this identity can perform this action against this resource in this context.

5
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:37.400
Role-based access control groups permissions into roles such as user, manager, auditor, or administrator.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.140
Groups can simplify assignment, especially when they come from an identity provider.

7
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.440
Claims and scopes can communicate identity attributes or delegated permissions in tokens.

8
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.540
These are useful inputs, but they must be interpreted carefully.

9
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:58.600
A claim that says department equals finance is not the same thing as permission to approve every finance action.

10
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.060
A scope that permits reading an API does not automatically decide which object instance may be read.

11
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.420
Object-level and function-level authorization are both critical.

12
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:15.400
Function-level checks decide whether an identity may perform a category of action, such as create users or export reports.

13
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:23.020
Object-level checks decide whether that identity may access this specific invoice, ticket, project, or customer record.

14
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.120
Enforcement belongs on the server side and should follow least privilege.

15
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:36.260
Client-side checks can improve usability, but they cannot be the security boundary because requests can be changed, replayed, or crafted directly.
